thank you I'm so happy to be here  tonight as the Governor's Award goes to  my friend great cinematographer Owen  royzman he will leave here tonight with  the Oscar he's so long deserved in 1971  when I was just out of college and  desperate to direct movies the French  Connection exploded into the world it  combined all the grit and grime and  handheld excitement of documentary  filmmaking with the narrative drive of  the best Hollywood thriller the genius  of that brew changed movies forever a  few there a few years later Owen and  Billy Friedkin collaborated again  working under very different conditions  they created a movie that scared the  pants off of everybody in the world The  Exorcist was shot on elaborate sets with  hundreds of in-camera effects along with  startling expressionistic confrontations  of darkness and light and yet people  sometimes forget the reality level of  the story is amplified by the hyper  realistic shots of Georgetown and  Washington that frame the harm  oh and Cha westerns and thrillers and  comedies sometimes he showed more than  one at the same time I'll give you an  example if you watch Network you see the  perfect marriage of theme and image  there's a great scene where the head of  the network Ned Beatty reads the bride  Act two anchorman Peter Finch in a long  conference room lit only by the lamps on  the table there's really been a stronger  evocation of God's wrath raining down on  a mere mortal with Sydney [ __ ] oh and  made Tootsie one of the funniest movies  of all times  it was chock-full of beautiful faces  sophisticated compositions and startling  reservoirs of emotion the remarkable  achievements kept coming three days of  the Condor straight time the electric  horseman true confessions taps absence  of malice we all wanted our movies to  look like that but Owen was something  doing something more than just  redefining the look of Hollywood films  he was inspiring young cinematographers  and filmmakers all over the world you  can see his influence in hundreds of  movies made since the French Connection  the private Owen royzman is funny and  generous and impossibly sweet Owen and I  made four movies together he was  nominated for wider when we were out  scouting those locations we would be  dazzled we were both not young men but  what we saw made us feel all the  excitement of young men when you get to  place figures in that magnificent  landscape to tell a big story about  America you know you've had a lucky life  Owen has inspired young filmmakers for  40 years he will inspire them forever  he is the great Owen Reisman  [Applause]
 

